@chapter ABI Formats and Assembler Syntax

@cindex ABI
@cindex assembler syntax
@cindex register usage

Every back end follows the same calling convention: a single value is
passed in the machine's first register and returned in the same
register; everything else travels through global variables.  See
@ref{Data and Parameters}.  This chapter records the assembler dialect,
register conventions, and invocation text for each supported processor.

@menu
* Common ABI::                  Shared conventions and naming
* 6502 Family ABI::             6502, 65c02, 65c816, HuC6280, RP2A03
* Z80 ABI::                     Zilog Z80
* SM83 ABI::                    Sharp SM83 (Game Boy)
* cp1610 ABI::                  Intellivision
* m6800 ABI::                   Motorola 6800
* m68k ABI::                    Motorola 68000
* i286 ABI::                    Intel 80286
* ARM7 ABI::                    ARM7TDMI (Game Boy Advance)
* F8 ABI::                      Fairchild F8 (Channel F)
@end menu

@node Common ABI
@section Common Conventions

@cindex method labels
@cindex OOPS
@cindex variable labels

@itemize
@item Method entry labels follow the OOPS convention
@code{Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}} on the 6502 family.
Hyphenated names translate to underscores (@code{Song--Heal--ID} emits
@code{Song_Heal_ID}).
@item Global variables are referred to by their derived assembly
symbols; class slots are fetched through the object pointer.
@item A method returns with the platform's return instruction
(@code{rts} on the 6502 family and Z80, @code{ret} on the i286 and
SM83, @code{pul pc} on the cp1610, @code{rts} on the m68k, and so on).
@end itemize

@node 6502 Family ABI
@section 6502, 65c02, 65c816, HuC6280, RP2A03

@cindex 64tass
@cindex 6502 registers

Output uses 64tass assembler syntax.  The assembler directives include
@code{.block}/@code{.bend} for local label scopes, @code{.byte} for data,
@code{.fill} for zero-initialized storage, and @code{.LogFault} for
runtime fault signaling.

@menu
* 6502 Registers and Calling Convention::
* 6502 AST Node Implementation::
* 6502 Memory Layout::
@end menu

@node 6502 Registers and Calling Convention
@subsection Register Conventions

@itemize
@item @code{A} (Accumulator) — the calling-convention register.  Single-byte
return values ride here; arguments are passed in A for unary calls.
@item @code{X} (Index) — subscript indices for array fetches, or temporary
storage during complex operations.  Holds the low byte when loading a
16-bit value in combination with A.
@item @code{Y} (Index) — slot offsets for object slot access.  The pair
@code{ldy #offset / lda (Self),y} fetches a slot of the current object.
@item @code{S} (Stack Pointer) — 6502 stack is 256 bytes, page 1.  Used for
@code{push/pull} during multi-byte arithmetic and for saving registers.
@end itemize

The OOPS method-label convention produces labels of the form
@code{Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}}.  Hyphenated COBOL names
become underscores: @code{Song--Heal--ID} maps to
@code{Song_Heal_ID}.

@node 6502 AST Node Implementation
@subsection AST Node Implementation

Each AST node type maps to 6502 assembly sequences as follows:

@itemize

@item @code{:move} — byte-by-byte copy via a loop when widths differ,
or @code{lda #imm / sta addr} for immediate-to-variable single bytes.
@code{MOVE NULL TO} pointer zeros the high byte.  Sign extension uses
@code{bpl} on the sign byte with subsequent zero-fill or $FF-fill.

@item @code{:add} — single-byte: @code{clc / adc rhs / sta result}.
Multi-byte: little-endian ADC loop from low to high, @code{clc} before
the first byte.  Optimizations: @code{ADD 1 TO var} emits @code{inc var};
@code{ADD -1} emits @code{dec var}; power-of-two adds use @code{asl a}
repeatedly in the accumulator.

@item @code{:subtract} — single-byte: @code{sec / sbc rhs / sta result}.
Multi-byte: little-endian SBC loop from low to high, @code{sec} before
the first byte.  Optimizations mirror ADD: @code{DEC} for subtracting 1.

@item @code{:compute} — delegates to @code{:add}/@code{:subtract} handlers
for expression evaluation, then stores the result.

@item @code{:if} — emits a compare chain that jumps to the ELSE label when
the condition is false.  Complex conditions use short-circuit logic:
@code{:and} evaluates left, then right if left succeeded; @code{:or}
evaluates left, jumps over right if left succeeded.

@item @code{:goto} — unconditional @code{jmp} (plain 6502) or @code{bra}
(65c02+).  @code{GO TO @dots{} DEPENDING ON} generates a trinary search
tree of @code{cmp}/@code{beq}/@code{blt}/@code{bge}.

@item @code{:perform} — @code{jsr paragraph} for out-of-line bodies;
inline bodies emit labeled loops with @code{djnz}, @code{cmp}, or
conditional branches for TIMES/UNTIL/VARYING variants.

@item @code{:invoke} — emits the Phantasia OOPS macro
@code{.CallMethod Call@var{Class}@var{Method}, @var{Class}Class} which
expands to @code{jsr DoCallMethod}.  The object pointer (usually
@code{Self}) and method table index are passed through global variables.

@item @code{:call} — bare @code{jsr @var{name}}.  Library calls emit
@code{jsr Lib.@var{name}}.  Far calls (cross-bank) emit
@code{.FarJSR @var{name}, @var{bank}}.

@item @code{:call-acc} — unary call with argument in A: loads A first,
then @code{jsr @var{name}} or @code{jsr Lib.@var{name}}.

@item @code{:set} — handles TO, UP BY, DOWN BY, TO NULL, TO ADDRESS OF,
TO SELF.  @code{SET id UP BY n} delegates to ADD; @code{SET id DOWN BY n}
delegates to SUBTRACT.

@item @code{:evaluate} — WHEN clauses emit compare-and-branch sequences.
WHEN OTHER falls through.  Each clause jumps to the end label after
executing its statements.

@item @code{:inspect} — TALLYING loops with Y-indexed memory scan;
CONVERTING scans and replaces matching bytes; REPLACING fills with a
constant byte via @code{djnz}.

@item @code{:string-blt} — Y-indexed block-transfer loop:
@code{ldy #0 / loop: lda src,y / sta dest,y / iny / cpy #len / bne loop}.

@item @code{:log-fault} — emits @code{.LogFault "@var{code}"}, a 4-character
fault identifier for the runtime fault handler.

@item @code{:debug-break} — emits @code{.DebugBreak} with the fault code.

@item @code{:goback}, @code{:exit-method}, @code{:exit-program},
@code{:stop-run} — all emit @code{rts}.

@item @code{:break}, @code{:continue} — emit jumps to the enclosing
PERFORM loop's break/continue labels (error if not inside a loop).

@end itemize

@node 6502 Memory Layout
@subsection Memory Layout

The 6502 backend uses little-endian byte ordering for multi-byte values
in memory.  Zero-page is reserved for temporary variables and pointers:

@itemize
@item @code{Pointer} / @code{Pointer+1} — 16-bit address for indirect
access via @code{(Pointer),y}.
@item @code{WorkALU} — temporary for in-memory ALU operations.
@item PIC scratch buffers (@code{PicS1_*}, @code{PicS2_*}) — for
decimal-scaled binary arithmetic.
@end itemize

BCD arithmetic: the @code{SED} instruction enables decimal mode for
@code{ADC}/@code{SBC}; @code{CLD} restores binary mode afterward.  The
RP2A03 lacks decimal mode, so BCD arithmetic uses software correction
(@code{daa}-style nibble adjustment after each binary addition).

CPU-specific instruction sets (64tass format):

@table @asis
@item @code{:6502}
Plain 6502: @code{jmp} for unconditional branches; @code{SED}/@code{CLD}
for BCD arithmetic; undocumented opcodes (@code{lax}, @code{dcp}) where
helpful and enabled by policy.
@item @code{:65c02}
Adds @code{bra}, @code{stz}, @code{trb}, @code{tsb}; no undocumented
opcodes.  @code{stz} clears memory without touching A.
@item @code{:65c816}
Like the 65c02 in emulation mode; native 24-bit addressing with
@code{.FarJSR}.  64tass syntax.
@item @code{:huc6280}
Like the 65c02; bank switching via macros.  Executes all 6502
instructions; delegates code generation to the 6502 backend.
@item @code{:rp2a03}
NES CPU.  6502 subset with no decimal mode: no @code{SED}; BCD and
packed-decimal arithmetic uses software correction routines instead
(binary ADC, then add 6 to the low nibble if it exceeds 9, and so on).
@end table

@node Z80 ABI
@section Zilog Z80

@cindex Z80 registers

Output is lowercase Z80 assembly in the common dialect (@code{ld a,(hl)},
@code{jr nz}, and so on).  Labels use PascalCase; paragraph names
derived from COBOL stabby-case retain hyphens.

@menu
* Z80 Registers and Calling Convention::
* Z80 AST Node Implementation::
* Z80 Memory Layout::
@end menu

@node Z80 Registers and Calling Convention
@subsection Register Conventions

@itemize
@item @code{A} (Accumulator) — the calling-convention register.  Single-byte
return values ride here; single-byte arguments are passed in A.
@item @code{HL} — 16-bit pointer and result register.  Used for 16-bit
arithmetic, addressing (@code{ld hl,(addr)}), and stack-relative access.
@item @code{BC} — count register for loops and string operations.
Holds loop counters; also used as a second 8-bit register via @code{B} alone.
@item @code{DE} — destination pointer and temporary.  Preferred over the
stack for holding intermediate 16-bit values.  @code{ex de,hl} swaps HL
and DE for common patterns.
@item @code{SP} (Stack Pointer) — 16-bit stack pointer.  Call/return uses
@code{call}/@code{ret}; 16-bit push/pop pairs preserve registers.
@item @code{IX}, @code{IY} — index registers for complex memory access.
Not used by generated code but available for hand-written additions.
@end itemize

Method labels follow the same OOPS convention as the 6502:
@code{Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}}.

@node Z80 AST Node Implementation
@subsection AST Node Implementation

Each AST node type maps to Z80 assembly sequences as follows:

@itemize

@item @code{:move} — single-byte: @code{ld a,(src) / ld (dest),a}.
16-bit: @code{ld hl,(src) / ld (dest),hl}.  BCD-to-binary and
binary-to-BCD conversions use @code{rrca}/@code{daa} sequences for
packed-decimal moves.

@item @code{:add} — single-byte: @code{ld b,a / ld a,(src) / add a,b / ld (dest),a}.
16-bit: @code{ld hl,to / ex de,hl / ld hl,from / add hl,de / ld (dest),hl}.
BCD mode uses the @code{daa} instruction after each @code{adc}.

@item @code{:subtract} — single-byte: @code{ld b,a / ld a,minuend / sub subtrahend / ld (dest),a}.
16-bit: uses @code{or a} to clear carry, then @code{sbc hl,de} for
@code{minuend - subtrahend}.  BCD mode applies nibble correction after
@code{sbc}.

@item @code{:compute} — evaluates the expression via @code{compile-z80-load}
into A (width=1) or HL (width=2), then stores to the target.

@item @code{:if} — emits a compare that jumps to the ELSE label when
false.  Two-byte compares push HL, load the other operand into DE, and
use @code{sbc hl,de} to compare.  Single-byte compares use @code{cp}
and branch accordingly.  Short-circuit @code{:and}/@code{:or} follow the
same pattern as the 6502 backend.

@item @code{:goto} — unconditional @code{jp @var{label}}.  DEPENDING ON
generates a compare chain with @code{cp n / jp z,@dots{}}.

@item @code{:perform} — @code{call paragraph} for out-of-line bodies.
Inline bodies emit labeled loops: TIMES uses B as counter with @code{djnz};
UNTIL tests at loop start; VARYING increments the variable after each
iteration.

@item @code{:invoke} — emits @code{call Invoke@var{Class}@var{Method}}.
For @code{:returning}, stores the result from A (single byte) or HL
(16-bit) to the target variable.

@item @code{:call} — bare @code{call @var{name}}.  Library calls emit
@code{call Lib.@var{name}}.

@item @code{:call-acc} — loads the argument into A, then @code{call}.

@item @code{:set} — handles TO, UP BY, DOWN BY, TO ADDRESS OF, TO SELF.
Delegates to ADD/SUBTRACT handlers for the UP BY/DOWN BY variants.

@item @code{:evaluate} — WHEN clauses emit compare-and-branch sequences
using @code{cp} for single bytes or @code{sbc hl,de} for 16-bit subjects.
WHEN OTHER falls through.  WHEN ANY always executes.  THROUGH clauses
test both lower and upper bounds.

@item @code{:inspect} — TALLYING scans memory with HL and increments a
counter variable; CONVERTING scans and replaces matching bytes;
REPLACING fills a region with a constant byte using @code{djnz}.

@item @code{:string-blt} — Z80 block-transfer loop:
@code{ld hl,src / ld de,dest / ld bc,len / loop: ld a,(hl) / ld (de),a /
inc hl / inc de / dec bc / ld a,b / or c / jr nz,loop}.

@item @code{:shift-left} — for single bytes: load into A, then @code{add a,a}
repeatedly.  For 16-bit values loaded into HL, left shift uses
@code{add hl,hl} or the @code{sla hl} undocumented instruction.

@item @code{:shift-right} — @code{srl a} for unsigned, @code{sra a} for
signed, repeated for multi-bit shifts.

@item @code{:log-fault} — emits a comment; the actual fault mechanism is
platform-defined.

@item @code{:goback}, @code{:exit-method}, @code{:exit-program},
@code{:stop-run}, @code{:exit} — all emit @code{ret}.

@item @code{:break}, @code{:continue} — emit @code{jp @var{label}} to
the enclosing PERFORM loop's break/continue label.

@end itemize

@node Z80 Memory Layout
@subsection Memory Layout

The Z80 backend uses little-endian byte ordering for 16-bit values.
There is no dedicated zero-page; temporary 16-bit storage is allocated
in the data segment.  The stack grows downward from high memory.

String and array operands are addressed via HL pointing into the data
section.  The BC register holds counts for block operations.

BCD arithmetic on the Z80 uses the @code{daa} instruction for packed
decimal correction after each addition or subtraction.  Binary-to-BCD
conversion uses a divide-by-10 loop with @code{sbc hl,de} and nibble
assembly.

@node SM83 ABI
@section Sharp SM83 (Game Boy)

@cindex RGBDS
@cindex SM83 registers

Output is RGBDS assembler syntax (@code{ld a,[hl]}, @code{jr nz}, and so
on).  The register conventions mirror the Z80: @code{A} accumulator,
@code{HL} pointer/result, @code{BC} count, @code{DE} destination/temp.

@subsubsection Register Conventions

@table @asis
@item @code{A}
Accumulator and calling-convention register.  Holds 8-bit values; paired
with @code{H} and @code{L} for 16-bit operations.
@item @code{HL}
16-bit pointer and result register.  Used for address calculations and
16-bit load/store operations.
@item @code{BC}
Count register, typically for loop counters or subscript indices.
@item @code{DE}
Destination pointer and temporary register.  Preferred over stack for
passing addresses.
@end table

@subsubsection Calling Conventions

@itemize
@item Methods are invoked via @code{call Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}}.
@item Return value rides in @code{A} (8-bit) or @code{HL} (16-bit).
@item Methods return with @code{ret}.
@item Local calls use @code{call} directly; library calls emit
@code{call CallLib@var{Name}}; banked calls emit
@code{call @var{Bank}:@var{Name}}.
@end itemize

@subsubsection AST Implementation

The SM83 backend dispatches through the generic @code{compile-statement}
function.  The main entry point @code{compile-to-assembly} produces
RGBDS @code{SECTION} directives and emits methods with labels of the form
@code{Method@var{Class}@var{Method}}.  A custom entry label (from
@code{:assembly-entry}) precedes the dispatch label, and both receive a
@code{::} suffix for RGBDS visibility.

Expression loading via @code{compile-sm83-load} emits either
@code{ld a,} for 8-bit or @code{ld hl,} for 16-bit values.  The backend
supports subscripted access (index scaled via @code{BC}), @code{:of}
slot-of-self fetches (base address in @code{HL}, offset added), and
bitwise operations (@code{and}, @code{or}, @code{xor}, @code{cpl}).

Conditions use @code{cp} (compare to @code{B}) for 8-bit or @code{sbc}
with @code{HL} for 16-bit, branching via @code{jr nz} or @code{jr z}.
Evaluation (EVALUATE statement) generates a chain of @code{cp}/@code{jr}
pairs with local labels of the form @code{.when@n}.

Multi-byte BCD arithmetic uses the @code{daa} instruction after each
add/subtract byte, looping over the operand bytes from low to high with
@code{adc} (add with carry) for subsequent bytes.

@node cp1610 ABI
@section Intellivision cp1610

@cindex as1600
@cindex cp1610 registers

Output is as1600 assembler syntax.  The cp1610 is a 16-bit RISC-like CPU
with eight general-purpose registers.

@subsubsection Register Conventions

@table @asis
@item @code{R0}–@code{R7}
16-bit general-purpose registers.
@item @code{R5}
Return address register for @code{JSR R5}.  Subroutine calls push the
return address into R5; the called routine returns via @code{JR R5}.
@item @code{R6}
Stack pointer.
@item @code{R0}
Calling-convention register.  8-bit EIGHTBOL values occupy the low byte.
@end table

@subsubsection Calling Conventions

@itemize
@item Methods are invoked via @code{JSR R5, Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}}.
@item Arguments and return values ride in R0 (low byte for 8-bit values).
@item Methods return with @code{JR R5}.
@item @code{:assembly-entry} on the first statement creates a custom entry
label that is equated to the dispatch label.
@end itemize

@subsubsection Instruction Set

The backend uses the classic cp1610 instruction mnemonics:

@table @asis
@item @code{MVII #imm, R@n}
Move immediate 16-bit value into register.
@item @code{MVI addr, R@n}
Load 16-bit value from memory into register.
@item @code{MVO R@n, addr}
Store register value to memory.
@item @code{ADDR R@m, R@n}
Add register to register (R@n += R@m).
@item @code{SUBR R@m, R@n}
Subtract register from register (R@n -= R@m).
@item @code{MOVR R@m, R@n}
Copy register to register.
@item @code{CMPR R@m, R@n}
Compare registers (sets flags from R@m - R@n).
@item @code{TSTR R@n}
Test register for zero.
@item @code{SLL / SARC}
Shift left logical / arithmetic right shift (for bit operations).
@item @code{JR R5}
Return from subroutine.
@end table

@subsubsection AST Implementation

The cp1610 backend is implemented in @code{src/backend-cp1610/backend-cp1610.lisp}.
The @code{compile-to-assembly} function expects a @code{:program} AST node and
loads copybook tables before emitting code.  It outputs a @code{ROMW 16} header
followed by @code{PROC}/@code{ENDP} pairs for each method.

Method labels follow the pattern @code{Method@var{Class}@var{Method}} in PascalCase
(hyphens removed, segments concatenated).  A custom assembly entry from
@code{:assembly-entry} replaces the dispatch label; the dispatch label is then
equated to it.

Expression loading via @code{compile-cp1610-load} handles:
@itemize
@item Immediate values via @code{MVII #, R0}
@item Memory variables via @code{MVI @var{Name}, R0}
@item Subscripted access: index in R0 scaled for element size, base address
loaded to R4, then indirect load via @code{MVI@ R4, R0}
@item Slot-of-self: slot offset added to @code{Self} pointer in R4
@end itemize

Conditions are evaluated in @code{compile-cp1610-condition}.  Comparison
operators (=, ≠, <, >, ≤, ≥) load both operands into R0/R1, emit @code{CMPR R1, R0},
and branch based on flags (@code{BEQ}, @code{BNEQ}, @code{BLT}, @code{BLE},
@code{BGT}, @code{BGE}).  Boolean conditions (@code{:is-zero},
@code{:is-not-zero}) use @code{TSTR R0}.

BCD arithmetic for the cp1610 uses software correction routines that adjust
each nybble after addition or subtraction, implemented in
@code{%cp1610-bcd-correct}.  Shift operations emit @code{SLL} (shift left)
or @code{SARC} (arithmetic shift right) counts via bit manipulation.

@node m6800 ABI
@section Motorola 6800

@cindex m6800
@cindex Vectrex

A minimal m6800 emitter intended for Vectrex-style Motorola-family
workflows.  Uses plain 6800 mnemonics (@code{LDAA}, @code{STAA},
@code{CMPA}, @code{BEQ}, @code{BNE}, @code{JSR}, @code{JMP}, @code{RTS})
with direct symbols for generated labels.

@subsubsection Register Conventions

@table @asis
@item @code{A}
8-bit accumulator.  The calling-convention register.
@item @code{X}
16-bit index register.  Used for addressing and loop counters.
@item @code{SP}
Stack pointer.
@end table

@subsubsection Calling Conventions

@itemize
@item Methods are invoked via @code{JSR Method@var{Class}@var{Method}}.
@item Return value rides in @code{A}.
@item Methods return with @code{RTS}.
@end itemize

@subsubsection AST Implementation

The m6800 backend follows the same pattern as the other backends: the
@code{compile-to-assembly} function produces methods with PascalCase labels
of the form @code{Method@var{Class}@var{Method}}, with @code{:assembly-entry}
creating a custom entry label.  Expression loading emits @code{LDAA} for
immediates or memory loads, and the condition compilation follows the same
branch-on-inverse logic used throughout Eightbol.

@node m68k ABI
@section Motorola 68000

@cindex 68000
@cindex m68k registers

Targets the Motorola 68000 instruction set only (not 68010, 68020, etc.).
Output uses GAS/AT&T-style assembly with the @code{move} family of
instructions and the 68000-specific branch instructions.

@subsubsection Register Conventions

@table @asis
@item @code{D0}–@code{D7}
Data registers.  @code{D0} is the calling-convention register (accumulator).
@item @code{A0}–@code{A7}
Address registers.  @code{A7} is the stack pointer.
@item @code{D1}
Temporary register for expression evaluation.
@item @code{A0}
Address temporary, used for pointer arithmetic in slot-of-self fetches and
subscripted access.
@end table

@subsubsection Calling Conventions

@itemize
@item Methods are invoked via @code{jsr Method@var{Class}@var{Method}}.
@item Return value rides in @code{D0}.
@item Methods return with @code{rts}.
@item Local calls use @code{jsr} directly; banked calls emit
@code{jsr @var{Bank}:@var{Name}}.
@end itemize

@subsubsection Instruction Set

The backend emits 68000 instructions in AT&T syntax (@code{move.b},
@code{move.w}, @code{add.w}, etc.).  Sizes are denoted with suffixes:
@code{.b} (byte), @code{.w} (word), @code{.l} (long).

@table @asis
@item @code{move.@var{sz} @var{src}, @var{dst}}
General move; source and destination separated by comma.
@item @code{add.@var{sz} @var{src}, @var{dst}}
Add source to destination register or memory.
@item @code{sub.@var{sz} @var{src}, @var{dst}}
Subtract source from destination.
@item @code{cmp.@var{sz} @var{src}, @var{dst}}
Compare (sets flags from dst - src).
@item @code{bra / bne / beq / bge / ble / blt / bgt}
Unconditional and conditional branches (word displacement).
@item @code{jsr / jmp / rts}
Jump to subroutine, jump, return from subroutine.
@item @code{lea @var{addr}, %a0}
Load effective address into A0 (used for pointer setup).
@end table

@subsubsection AST Implementation

The m68k backend is implemented in @code{src/backend-m68k/backend-m68k.lisp}.
The @code{compile-to-assembly} function produces a @code{.text} section header
and emits method labels with PascalCase names of the form
@code{Method@var{Class}@var{Method}}.

Expression loading via @code{compile-m68k-load} emits @code{move.@var{sz} #val, %d0}
for immediates, @code{move.@var{sz} @var{name}, %d0} for variables, or
complex sequences for slots and subscripts.  The backend uses the helper
@code{m68k-size-suffix} to convert width (1 for byte, 2 for word) to the
appropriate @code{.b} or @code{.w} suffix.

Slot-of-self access loads the object pointer via @code{move.l Self, %a0},
then accesses the slot at an offset with @code{move.@var{sz} @var{offset}(%a0), %d0}.
Subscripted access computes the element address in @code{%a0} by scaling the
index (add.w %d0, %d0 for 16-bit elements) and loading the base address.

Conditions in @code{compile-m68k-condition} emit a @code{cmp.@var{sz}} and
branch on the inverse condition (@code{bne} when the true branch should be
taken if equal).  EVALUATE generates a chain of comparisons with local
labels of the form @code{.L@n}.

BCD arithmetic uses the 68000's built-in @code{abcd} (add decimal) and
@code{sbcd} (subtract decimal) instructions on byte operands, with
@code{moveq #0, %%d2} and @code{move %%ccr, %%d2} to manage the extend
bit between multi-byte operations.

@node i286 ABI
@section Intel 80286

@cindex 16-bit
@cindex Intel syntax

A 16-bit backend producing Intel-syntax assembly (@code{mov ax, bx},
@code{mov al, [si]}).  Registers: @code{AX} accumulator and calling-
convention register, @code{BX} temp, @code{SI} index, @code{DI}
destination pointer, @code{CX} count; segments @code{CS}, @code{DS},
@code{SS}, @code{ES}.  Control: @code{call}, @code{ret}, @code{jmp},
@code{jz}, @code{jnz}.

@node ARM7 ABI
@section ARM7TDMI (Game Boy Advance)

@cindex Thumb
@cindex GAS
@cindex armv4t

Targets the Game Boy Advance ARM7TDMI (@code{armv4t}).  Thumb is the
default code path for size, using unified GAS syntax with
@code{.thumb}, @code{.syntax unified}, and @code{.cpu arm7tdmi}.
Instructions used: @code{movs}, @code{ldr}/@code{ldrb}/@code{ldrh},
@code{str}/@code{strb}/@code{strh}, @code{add}, @code{subs}, @code{cmp},
@code{b}/@code{bl}/@code{bne}@dots{}, @code{bx lr}.  Register usage:
@code{r0} is the value and calling-convention register; @code{r1},
@code{r2}, @code{r3} are scratch and address registers.

@subsubsection Register Conventions

@table @asis
@item @code{r0}
Calling-convention register.  Holds argument values and return values.
@item @code{r1}–@code{r3}
Scratch registers and address temporaries.  Used for expression evaluation
and pointer setup.
@item @code{r13} (SP)
Stack pointer (managed by the runtime).
@item @code{r14} (LR)
Link register, holds return address from @code{bl} calls.
@item @code{r15} (PC)
Program counter.
@end table

@subsubsection Directives

Every method in the ARM7 backend is preceded by the @code{.thumb_func}
directive.  This tells the assembler (and linker) that the symbol is a
Thumb function entry point, which is required for correct interworking
between ARM and Thumb code:

@example
        .thumb_func
MethodGameHeroHealth:
        @@ ... method body ...
        bx      lr
@end example

The header emitted at the start of each compilation unit:

@example
        .arch   armv4t
        .cpu    arm7tdmi
        .thumb
        .syntax unified
@end example

@subsubsection Calling Conventions

@itemize
@item Methods are invoked via @code{bl Method@var{Class}@var{Method}}.
@item Return value rides in @code{r0}.
@item Methods return with @code{bx lr} (branch-exchange to link register).
@item @code{:assembly-entry} on the first statement creates a custom entry
label before the dispatch label, both marked with @code{.thumb_func}.
@end itemize

@subsubsection AST Implementation

The ARM7 backend is implemented in @code{src/backend-arm7/backend-arm7.lisp}.
The @code{compile-to-assembly} function emits the architecture and syntax
directives, then iterates over methods.  Each method body is compiled by
@code{compile-arm7-method}, which wraps the body with @code{.thumb_func}
and an optional custom entry label.

Expression loading via @code{compile-arm7-load} handles:
@itemize
@item Immediate values via @code{movs r0, #@var{val}}
@item Memory variables via @code{ldr r2, =@var{name}} followed by
@code{ldrb/ldrh r0, [r2]}
@item Slot-of-self: loads @code{Self} pointer into r2, then accesses
at offset with @code{ldrb r0, [r2, #@var{offset}]}
@item Subscripted access: index in r0 scaled by element size, base
address in r2, then @code{ldrb/ldrh r0, [r2, r0]}
@end itemize

Bitwise operations use the Thumb-specific suffixed forms (@code{ands},
@code{orrs}, @code{eors}, @code{mvns}) that update condition flags.

Conditions in @code{compile-arm7-condition} emit @code{cmp r0, r1} after
loading operands into r0 and r1, then branch with the inverse condition
(@code{bne} when the true branch should be taken if equal).  EVALUATE
generates a chain of comparisons with local labels of the form
@code{.L@n}.

BCD arithmetic for ARM7 uses software correction sequences: after each
@code{adds} or @code{subs}, the code checks and adjusts each nibble
(@code{and r1, r0, #0x0F}; @code{cmp r1, #10}; @code{addge r0, r0, #6};
and similar for the high nibble with @code{0xA0} and @code{0x60}).  The
backend's @code{compile-arm7-add} and @code{compile-arm7-subtract}
implement this for multi-byte operands.

Break and continue in inline PERFORM loops are handled by dynamic
variables @code{*arm7-break-label*} and @code{*arm7-continue-label*}
that capture the loop's end and continue labels respectively.

@node F8 ABI
@section Fairchild F8 (Channel F)

@cindex F8
@cindex Channel F

Emits Fairchild F8 assembly with the Turner/Fairchild mnemonics: the
@code{A} accumulator, @code{DC0} data counter, @code{LM}/@code{ST},
@code{DCI}, @code{LI}/@code{AI}/@code{CI}, @code{LR H/DC0}, branches
@code{BR}/@code{BZ}/@code{BNZ}@dots{}, and subroutine call/return
@code{PI}/@code{POP}.  16-bit values use scratch 10 (lo) and 11 (hi);
scratch 6--9 are code-generation temporaries.

@subsubsection Register Conventions

@table @asis
@item @code{A}
8-bit accumulator.  The calling-convention register; holds 8-bit values.
@item @code{DC0}
16-bit data counter (address register).  Used for memory accesses via
@code{LM} (load via DC0) and @code{ST} (store via DC0).
@item @code{DC1}
Secondary 16-bit data counter (available for string operations).
@item @code{H} (hidden pointer)
An implicit 16-bit pointer into memory adjacent to DC0; accessed via
@code{LR H, DC0} (copy DC0 to H) and @code{LR DC0, H} (copy H to DC0).
@item @code{Q} (hidden counter)
An implicit counter used by @code{LM} and @code{ST} for automatic
increment after each access.
@item @code{IS} (index stack pointer)
Points to the index stack; @code{LR} instructions access scratch registers.
@item @code{PC}
Program counter.
@item @code{DC}
Data counter of the system bus.
@item @code{AC} (arithmetic unit counter)
Controls the arithmetic unit.
@end table

@subsubsection Scratch Registers

The F8 has an index stack with 16 scratch registers (0–15) accessible via
@code{LR} (load register) instructions:

@table @asis
@item @code{0}–@code{5}
General-purpose temporaries.
@item @code{6}
Array index for subscripted access.
@item @code{7}
Temporary, used as loop count in string operations.
@item @code{8}–@code{9}
Temporaries for boolean and comparison operations.
@item @code{10}
Low byte of 16-bit temporaries (used with register 11 for hi).
@item @code{11}
High byte of 16-bit temporaries (used with register 10 for lo).
@item @code{12}–@code{13}
Temporaries, used for 16-bit arithmetic intermediate values.
@end table

@subsubsection Instruction Set

@table @asis
@item @code{LI @var{imm}}
Load immediate into accumulator.
@item @code{LM}
Load memory byte via DC0, increment DC0.
@item @code{ST}
Store accumulator to memory via DC0, increment DC0.
@item @code{DCI @var{addr}}
Load immediate address into DC0.
@item @code{LR @var{n}, A}
Load from scratch register n into accumulator.
@item @code{LR A, @var{n}}
Load accumulator into scratch register n.
@item @code{LR @var{n}, H}
Copy DC0 low byte to scratch register n (for pointer save/restore).
@item @code{LR H, DC0}
Copy DC0 low byte from H hidden register to DC0.
@item @code{LR DC0, H}
Copy H hidden register to DC0 (high part from scratch).
@item @code{AS @var{n}}
Add scratch register n to accumulator (with carry).
@item @code{ADC}
Add with carry from extended bit.
@item @code{AI @var{imm}}
Add immediate to accumulator.
@item @code{CI @var{imm}}
Compare immediate to accumulator.
@item @code{PI @var{addr}}
Program interrupt: call subroutine at address (pushes return address).
@item @code{POP}
Pop return address from stack and branch to it.
@item @code{JMP @var{addr}}
Unconditional jump.
@item @code{BR @var{addr}}
Branch (relative, short).
@item @code{BZ / BNZ @var{addr}}
Branch if accumulator is zero / not zero.
@item @code{BM / BP @var{addr}}
Branch if minus / plus (sign bit).
@item @code{BZ / BNZ / BNC}
Branch if zero / not zero / no carry.
@item @code{SL 1 / SR 1}
Shift left / shift right by one bit.
@item @code{COM}
Complement accumulator (logical NOT).
@item @code{INC / DS @var{n}}
Increment accumulator / decrement scratch register n.
@item @code{NI / OI / XI}
Logical AND / OR / XOR with immediate.
@item @code{NS @var{n}}
Logical AND accumulator with scratch register n.
@item @code{XS @var{n}}
Logical XOR accumulator with scratch register n.
@item @code{LNK}
Link: add carry bit to accumulator (used after ADC).
@end table

@subsubsection Calling Conventions

@itemize
@item Methods are invoked via @code{PI Method@var{Class}@var{Method}}.
@item Return value rides in @code{A}.
@item Methods return with @code{POP}.
@item @code{:assembly-entry} on the first statement creates a custom entry
label before the dispatch label.
@end itemize

@subsubsection AST Implementation

The F8 backend is implemented in @code{src/backend-f8/backend-f8.lisp}.
The @code{compile-to-assembly} function expects a @code{:program} AST node
and loads copybook tables before emitting code.

Method labels follow the pattern @code{Method@var{Class}@var{Method}} in
PascalCase.  A custom assembly entry from @code{:assembly-entry} replaces
the dispatch label; the dispatch label is then emitted on the line after
the custom entry.

Expression loading via @code{compile-f8-load} handles:
@itemize
@item Immediate 8-bit values via @code{LI @var{val}}.
@item Immediate 16-bit values via @code{LI} into scratch 10 (low), then
scratch 11 (high).
@item Memory variables: @code{DCI @var{name}} loads the address into DC0,
then @code{LM} loads the low byte (and a second @code{LM} for the high byte
for 16-bit values).
@item Subscripted access: index in scratch 6, base address via
@code{DCI}, then @code{%f8-dc0-bump-by-count} loops to advance DC0 by
the index.
@item Slot-of-self: @code{%f8-self-pointer-to-dc0} loads the Self pointer
into DC0 via two @code{LM} instructions and @code{LR 10/11, A}, then
@code{%f8-dc0-add-slot-offset} adds the slot offset.
@end itemize

16-bit arithmetic uses scratch 10 (low) and 11 (high) as the primary
temporary, with scratch 12-13 for intermediate values.  The helper
@code{%f8-add-16} emits the sequence @code{LR A, 10; AS 12; LR 10, A;
LR A, 11; LNK; AS 13; LR 11, A} to add @code{12:13} to @code{10:11}.
Negation uses @code{%f8-negate-16-10} for two's complement in place.

BCD arithmetic for F8 uses software correction because F8 lacks a
decimal-adjust instruction.  The backend implements this in
@code{compile-f8-add} and @code{compile-f8-subtract}: after each
byte-wise add or subtract with @code{AS} and @code{ASD} (add/subtract
with carry from extend bit), the code adjusts each nibble by testing and
adding $06 or $60 when necessary.

Conditions in @code{compile-f8-condition} use the F8's compare-immediate
(@code{CI}) and branch instructions.  For 8-bit comparisons, operands are
loaded into the accumulator and a scratch register, then @code{XS} (XOR)
and @code{BNZ} check equality, or the accumulator is compared directly.
For 16-bit comparisons, the backend uses the subtraction-with-borrow
sequence and checks the high byte for sign (@code{BM}) or zero (@code{BZ}
plus @code{BP}).

EVALUATE generates a chain of comparisons with local labels of the form
@code{_@var{prefix}@var{n}} (e.g., @code{_eval3}).  Each @code{WHEN}
clause emits a @code{:is-zero} test for the null terminator, then
compares the subject to the phrase expression and branches to the next
label on mismatch.

@node Error Conditions
@appendix Error Conditions and Fault Codes

@cindex errors
@cindex faults
@cindex conditions

This appendix lists the compiler's error conditions and the runtime
fault mechanism.

@menu
* Compiler Errors::             Compile-time condition hierarchy
* Runtime Faults::              The fault system and fault codes
@end menu

@node Compiler Errors
@section Compiler Errors

All compiler errors are subtypes of @code{compiler-error} and carry a
@code{message}.  The hierarchy, from most to least specific:

@table @asis
@item @code{source-error}
A source-level error: a parser or unsupported-statement failure.  Reports
the file, line, and sequence number when known.
@item @code{lexer-error}
A lexing failure; reports the file and line plus the offending form.
@item @code{copybook-error}
A copybook failure.  Subtypes: @code{copybook-not-found},
@code{copybook-invalid-name}, @code{copybook-read-error}.
@item @code{backend-error}
A code-generation failure for a specific CPU.  Subtypes:
@code{backend-ast-error} (unexpected AST shape),
@code{backend-condition-not-implemented},
@code{backend-string-blt-error},
@code{backend-call-service-error}, @code{backend-copy-not-expanded}.
@item @code{compile-error}
A pipeline failure; reports the compilation stage and input.  Subtypes:
@code{input-file-not-found}, @code{parse-failed-error},
@code{invalid-ast-error}, @code{undefined-class-reference},
@code{routine-not-terminated}.
@item @code{usage-error}
A command-line error.  Subtypes: @code{unknown-option-error},
@code{dangling-option-error}, @code{unknown-cpu-error}.
@end table

@node Runtime Faults
@section Runtime Faults

@cindex LOG FAULT
@cindex LogFault

At runtime, @code{LOG FAULT} (or @code{LogFault}) signals a fault
through the platform fault handler.  Fault codes are four-character
strings; the assembler renders them in the platform's minifont for the
debugger.  A @code{STOP RUN} halts the program.  On the 6502 family a
@code{BREAK} statement fires the processor's @code{brk} to enter the
monitor.

